    "2026-11-05:core_research": {
      "status": "prepared",
      "updated_at": "2026-11-05T06:12:48.464213+00:00",
      "attempts": 1,
      "pending": {
        "topic": "efficient_ai",
        "title": "高效 AI、模型压缩与端侧适配",
        "url": "https://vorasio.com/posts/20261105-core_research.html",
        "created_at": "2026-11-05T06:12:48.464213+00:00",
        "items": [
          {
            "uid": "ce9f8f3fe2d7cb9347d6cc67",
            "title": "Balancing hierarchical representation and quantization: A performance trade-off in text embeddings",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06859v1",
            "published": "2026-11-04T18:15:08+00:00",
            "summary": "Hierarchical text embedding models offer variable-length representations, while quantization reduces numerical precision; both aim to improve computational efficiency. This work is an empirical benchmark study of how these two orthogonal, already-established techniques interact. We evaluate 38 tasks from the Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (MTEB) using four transformer-based embedding models, covering 1D and 2D hierarchical representations combined with 32-bit, 8-bit, and binary quantization, for 168 configurations per task. We report each efficiency dimension separately: theoretical per-vector storage and end-to-end evaluation time. Under a Friedman test with Nemenyi post-hoc analysis, int8 quantization is statistically indistinguishable from float32 for pair classification and re-ranking and costs at most 1.5 percentage points overall, whereas binary quantization is significantly worse across every task category. Halving the embedding dimension once is not statistically detectable above $m=16$; two successive halvings always are. Combining an 8x dimensionality reduction with int8 yields a 32x reduction in per-vector storage at 94.4% of baseline effectiveness. Because evaluation times were measured once, we treat them as indicative: we find no evidence that quantization speeds up evaluation, whereas sampling from intermediate layers shortens it substantially. Retrieval degrad",
            "source": "arXiv Efficient AI",
            "kind": "research",
            "doi": "",
            "date_precision": "day"
          },
          {
            "uid": "df855f571dbd659f64fd7fcc",
            "title": "CompressionBench: How can your coding agents compress LLMs?",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06858v1",
            "published": "2026-11-04T18:14:50+00:00",
            "summary": "Recursive self-improvement requires AI systems to improve the models and infrastructure used by subsequent systems. LLM compression offers a concrete efficiency objective: retain a pretrained model's behavior within a smaller model storage budget. We study whether coding agents can carry out this step autonomously. We formulate compression as a terminal task in which an agent receives a source checkpoint, storage limit, inference environment, and time budget, then constructs compressed weights and executable inference code. The agent controls its algorithm and experiments; perplexity feedback guides search, and independent verification checks the final model. Across six agents and six model--size settings, 29 of 36 submissions pass verification. Selecting the lowest-perplexity agent design per task yields an average 3.4% perplexity increase over BF16 and 77.33% downstream accuracy, compared with 79.56% for BF16. Recorded experiments reveal how agents achieve these outcomes and where improvement breaks down. Agents lower PPL in 21 of 24 revisions at nearly fixed storage; complete-model feedback reverses 17 of 79 prior local rankings; and generation and inference tests expose failures that likelihood alone misses. The results identify representation design, experimental judgment, and capability validation as central to agent-driven model compression, a measurable component of eff",
            "source": "arXiv Efficient AI",
            "kind": "research",
            "doi": "",
            "date_precision": "day"
          },
          {
            "uid": "7fb408e31af4618d63dbc22a",
            "title": "VA-TDM: A Unified View for Improving Few-Step Diffusion Models by Velocity Adversarial Distillation",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06796v1",
            "published": "2026-11-04T17:14:20+00:00",
            "summary": "Few-step distillation offers efficient generation with diffusion and flow models, but preserving quality with only a few sampling steps remains challenging. Distribution-matching methods via score distillation provide a strong foundation, yet they rely on accurately tracking the student's changing distribution. At intermediate generation steps in few-step sampling, student-score estimation can be difficult, and commonly used estimators may describe a different distribution from the one used to update the student. Flow matching allows a more flexible approach to supervision through velocity fields constructed from the student's sampling process. Building on this perspective, we introduce Velocity Adversarial Distillation (VAD), which uses a learned critic to compare student and teacher fields and turn their disagreement into feedback for learning. VAD unifies score distillation, Fisher-based matching, and moment matching, also explaining existing heuristic MMD updates and delivering new training objectives. We further develop VA-TDM, which follows trajectory distribution matching: providing supervision for partial denoising along the student trajectory. Beyond this, VA-TDM combines transition-aware online velocity learning and dual-form VAD supervision across intermediate student transitions. Extensive results show that VA-TDM is powerful for learning few-step generators, achiev",
            "source": "arXiv Efficient AI",
            "kind": "research",
            "doi": "",
            "date_precision": "day"
          }
        ],
        "notify": false,
        "mode": "production",
